    {
      "id": "7molpbp",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. BASIC CONCEPT"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Mastering conversion rules between fractions, decimals, and percentages is essential for speed math.",
        "Memorizing standard percentage values accelerates calculation speed during aptitude tests.",
        "Percentage increase and decrease concepts form the foundation for profit, loss, and discount problems.",
        "Successive percentage change applies to cumulative variations like compound interest and consecutive discounts.",
        "Reverse percentage techniques help calculate the original value when given a modified final value."
      ],
      "id": "s9hcq8i"
    },
    {
      "id": "bvxc5sa",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Percentage means 'per hundred'.<br></br>1% = 1/100",
        "Percentage is used to express increase or decrease, comparison, profit and loss, marks, population, growth, etc.<br></br><br></br><em>In SBIexamination, percentage questions are direct, formula-based, and often linked with Ratio, Profit–Loss and Simple Interest.</em>"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "dhcbaam",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "2. CONVERSION RULES"
    },
    {
      "id": "nj3fk2s",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "Fraction to Percentage:<br></br>(a/b) × 100<br></br>Example: 3/5 = 60%",
        "Percentage to Fraction:<br></br>x% = x/100<br></br>Example: 25% = 1/4",
        "Decimal to Percentage:<br></br>Multiply the decimal by 100<br></br>Example: 0.75 = 75%"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "lowdqn8",
      "type": "heading",
